<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Sarcoma</journal-id><journal-title>Sarcoma</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1357-714X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1369-1643</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">18521397</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2395608</article-id>            <article-id pub-id-type="pii">S1357714X0400012X</article-id>            <article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1080/13577140410001710521</article-id>      <article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>High-Dose Chemotherapy Followed by Peripheral and/or Bone Marrow Stem Cell Transplant in Patients With Advanced Sarcoma: Experience of a Canadian Centre</article-title></title-group><contrib-group>            <contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Hotte</surname><given-names>S&#x000e9;bastien J.</given-names></name><address><email>sebastien.hotte@hrcc.on.ca</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib>            <contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>Anne M.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib>            <contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bramwell</surname><given-names>Vivien H.C.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib>            <contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Howson-Jan</surname><given-names>Kang</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib>      </contrib-group>            <aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>                <addr-line>Department of Medicine</addr-line>                <addr-line>McMaster University and Division of Medical Oncology</addr-line>                <addr-line>Hamilton Regional Cancer Centre (HRCC)</addr-line>                <addr-line>699 Concession Street</addr-line>                <addr-line>Hamilton</addr-line>                <addr-line>Ontario</addr-line>                <addr-line>L8V 5C2</addr-line>                <country>Canada</country>            </aff>            <aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>                <addr-line>Department of Oncology</addr-line>                <addr-line>Queen's University and Division of Medical Oncology</addr-line>                <addr-line>Kingston Regional Cancer Centre (KRCC)</addr-line>                <addr-line>Kingston</addr-line>                <addr-line>Ontario</addr-line>                <country>Canada</country>            </aff>            <aff id="aff3"><label>3</label>                <addr-line>Department of Medicine</addr-line>                <addr-line>University of Western Ontario</addr-line>                <addr-line>Division of Medical Oncology</addr-line>                <addr-line>LRCC and Division of Hematology, London Health Science Centre</addr-line>                <addr-line>London</addr-line>                <addr-line>Ontario</addr-line>                <country>Canada</country>            </aff>        <pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Jun-Sep</season><year>2004</year></pub-date><volume>8</volume><issue>2-3</issue><fpage>63</fpage><lpage>69</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 2004 Hindawi Publishing Corporation.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2004</copyright-year><copyright-holder>Hindawi Publishing Corporation</copyright-holder><license license-type="open-access" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/"><p>This is an open access article distributed under the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</p></license></permissions><abstract>            <p><italic>Purpose</italic>: Few reports have been published on the evaluation of stem cell auto transplantation for chemosensitive sarcomas.Some suggest benefit, others do not. We present results of 24 patients with sarcoma undergoing autotransplantation at aCanadian institution.</p>            <p><italic>Patients and Methods</italic>: Twenty-four patients were treated between 1988 and 1998: 23 were &#x02265;18 years (median 27; range 12&#x02013;56); genders were equal; 12 patients had Ewing's sarcoma. At diagnosis, 12 (50%) had metastatic disease. Prior toautotransplant, all had &#x02265;1 chemotherapy regimen. Fourteen (58%) were in complete remission (CR) and seven (29%) had minimal residual disease. All received etoposide 60 mg/kg (Day &#x02013;4), melphalan 140 mg/m&#x003b1;<sup>2</sup> on (Day &#x02013;3) and a stem cellreinfusion (Day 0).</p>            <p><italic>Results</italic>: Three patients (12.5%) were alive and disease-free with median follow-up of 92 months (80&#x02013;142); one was alivewith disease 32 months post-autotransplant. Twenty had died (disease, 17; transplant-related, 2; unknown, 1). Of the four alive, three had Ewing's sarcoma, one alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma, and all were in CR at transplant. Median time to relapsewas 6 months (2&#x02013;59). Sixteen of 18 (89%) relapsed within 1 year. Median overall survival was 10 months (0&#x02013;137). A trend towards improved survival (<italic>P</italic>=0.07) was evident for patients in CR prior to autotransplant.</p>            <p><italic>Conclusions</italic>: Stem cell autotransplantation does not benefit most patients with sarcoma. A subgroup of high-risk patients in CR may fare better and warrant further study.</p>        </abstract></article-meta></front></article>